      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a valid point, and it's a common critique that creators face when writing diverse characters. Some argue that using stereotypes can be a form of authenticity, as they reflect how people of color might experience the world. However, this approach often relies on a limited understanding of human complexity and ignores the danger of reinforcing systemic racism and erasure.\n\nFor example, writing a character from a particular neighborhood with tough, street-smart traits might indeed feel more authentic to that community, but it also risks making them a one-dimensional \"tough talk\" stereotype. This can perpetuate the very stereotypes that this approach seeks to subvert.\n\nIn reality, people are complex, multidimensional, and can't be reduced to a single archetype. By forcing characters to conform to certain roles, we risk creating bubbles of inauthenticity that isolate the audience from the people and experiences they're meant to represent.\n\nA better approach might be to subvert these stereotypes and add nuance to your characters' portrayals. For instance, you could make a character from a rough neighborhood not just tough, but also kind, calculating, or vulnerable in ways that defy expectations. This creates a more complex, relatable, and authentic portrayal of the character.",
